      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We retrospectively analyzed LMR at diagnosis and at completion of first-line therapy and prognosis in173 patients with DLBCL from 2005 to 2016. We found that patients with an LMR < 3.2 at diagnosis, as well as at completion of first-line therapy, had significantly lower PFS and OS rates than those with an LMR ≥ 3.2 (P<0.05). Patients with LMR that recovered from the low level at diagnosis showed superior overall survival (OS) (P=0.000) and progression-free survival (PFS) (P=0.001) compared with patients who failed to achieve a higher value at the completion of therapy. The multivariate analysis demonstrated that LMR values that did not increase upon completion of first-line therapy were an independent predictor for inferior OS (P=0.021) and PFS (P=0.046). In conclusion, LMR at diagnosis and at completion of first-line therapy is a simple biomarker to predict clinical outcomes in DLBCL. LMR recovery from low levels at diagnosis, irrespective of whether LMR reached the cutoff value, was associated with improved clinical outcomes."},
